<p>Pictures are made light for the web as they upload, so pages stay fast without anyone thinking about it.</p>
{{ shot( 'images', 'Media Library: a picture\'s details and its alternative text.' ) }}

<h3>What happens to an upload</h3>
<ul class="guide-list">
	<li><strong>Big photos are made smaller.</strong> Anything wider or taller than 1600 pixels is scaled down to 1600, which is plenty for any screen.</li>
	<li><strong>Pages use a WebP copy.</strong> A JPG or PNG is also saved as WebP, a much lighter format every browser shows. Your original file is kept.</li>
	<li><strong>Pictures already on the site do not change.</strong> Only new uploads are treated.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Upload a picture</h3>
<ol class="guide-steps">
	<li>Open <a href="{{ go.media }}"><strong>Media</strong></a> and click <strong>Add Media File</strong>, or drag the files onto the page.</li>
	<li>Click the picture to open its details.</li>
	<li>Fill in <strong>Alternative Text</strong>: one short sentence saying what the picture shows ("Volunteers sorting food at the spring drive"). Screen readers read it to blind visitors, and Google reads it too. Leave it empty only for a purely decorative picture.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Keep one picture exactly as it is</h3>
<p>A diagram, a logo or a picture with fine text can lose sharpness. To keep a file untouched (no resizing, no WebP), end its name with <code>-nr</code> <em>before</em> uploading it: <code>price-chart-nr.png</code>.</p>
<div class="guide-tip">Good habits: name files in plain words before uploading (<code>spring-food-drive.jpg</code>, not <code>IMG_4032.jpg</code>), and avoid uploading the same picture twice. The Media Library's search finds what is already there.</div>
